<exercises xml:id="ex-ParametricCurves" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Explain how a curve written in parametric form is different than one written in standard function notation.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                For each of the following parametric curves, plot the curves in the graphing utility below. Then, find at least 5 different points on the curve, plot them, and describe the curve.
            </p>
            <interactive platform="doenetml" width="95%" aspect="1:1">
                <slate surface="doenetml">
                    <xi:include parse="text" href="../doenet/ex-ParametricGraphingUtility.doenet"/>
                </slate>
            </interactive>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\begin{array}{l}
                        x=t-4\\
                        y=3+t-t^2
                        \end{array}
                    </m> for <m>0\leq t\leq 10</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\begin{array}{l}
                        x = t+3\\
                        y = 5-2t
                        \end{array}
                    </m> for <m>0\leq t\leq 5</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\begin{array}{l}
                        x=5\cos^2(t)\\
                        y=5\sin^2(t)
                        \end{array}
                    </m> for <m>0\leq t\leq 2\pi</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\begin{array}{l}
                        x=\sqrt{t+4}\\
                        y=5t+1
                        \end{array}
                    </m> for <m>0\leq t\leq 5</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Eliminate the parameter, <m>t</m>, in each of the parametric curves, expressing the curve either as a function <m>y=f(x)</m> or <m>x=f(y)</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\begin{array}{l}
                        x=t-4\\
                        y=3+t-t^2
                        \end{array}
                    </m> for <m>0\leq t\leq 10</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\begin{array}{l}
                        x = t+3\\
                        y = 5-2t
                        \end{array}
                    </m> for <m>0\leq t\leq 5</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\begin{array}{l}
                        x=5\cos^2(t)\\
                        y=5\sin^2(t)
                        \end{array}
                    </m> for <m>0\leq t\leq 2\pi</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\begin{array}{l}
                        x=\sqrt{t+4}\\
                        y=5t+1
                        \end{array}
                    </m> for <m>0\leq t\leq 5</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In <xref ref="act-CircleExploration" text="type-global-title"/>, we saw the we can define a circle parametrically by
                <md>
                    <mrow>x \amp = \cos(t)</mrow>
                    <mrow>y \amp = \sin(t)</mrow>
                </md>
                for <m>0\leq t \leq 2\pi</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Remind yourself why this parametric definition makes sense, based on how we think about trig functions on a unit circle.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Eliminate the parameter to create an equation with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> related to each other.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    Try squaring each parametric function! How can you combine them in a typical trigonometric identity way?
                </p>
            </hint>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How does the function <m>f(t)=\cos(5t)</m> compare to the function <m>g(t)=\cos(t)</m>? How about <m>\ell(t)=\sin(5t)</m> compared to the function <m>m(t)=\sin(t)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The parametric curve
                    <md>
                        <mrow>x \amp = \cos(5t)</mrow>
                        <mrow>y \amp = \sin(5t)</mrow>
                    </md>
                    for <m>0 \leq t \leq 2\pi</m> looks exactly the same as the unit circle we defined earlier.
                </p>
                <p>
                    How is it different? What does the coefficient on <m>t</m> do?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Create a parametric curve that looks like the unit circle but has an opposite orientation (direction).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</exercises>